BillLi wrote:

> The question is why the process refusing to exit? When the user logout, I
> would assume the script (parent) process will send the HUP signal to the
> child process and this "kill" signal should kill the child process. Looks
> like either the child process didn't receive this or the child process
> received the signal but something is running in a higher priority.

Any process can decide to ignore signalls - all, except SIGKILL, which is never 
received actually, but the kernel just kills the recipient of the signal. This 
override is done via a system call "sigaction()".

> Would this have anything to do with the kernel parameter settings e.g.
> semaphore that required to be tuned? Also, why the problem is only happening
> from time to time but can't be reproduced at will?

Two things. One - try to find out if your application is calling "sigaction()", 
either search the source or use "alpha-trace-1.34" (or whatever it is called) to 
trace system calls. Two - if it is in-house developed, get ahold of a developer, 
connect a debugger to such a process and see where it's looping (even "dbx" can 
do this, let alone "LaDebug").

Nix.



