"JF Larvoire" <jean-francois.larvoire@remove_this.free.fr> writes:

> we'd like to measure the duration of very short code pieces.  Is
> there a way to get access to processor clock counters on the Alpha?

Yes. Try this code:

#ifdef __GNUC__
static inline unsigned realcc(void) {
    unsigned long cc;
    asm volatile("rpcc %0" : "=r"(cc));
    return cc;
}
#else  /* DEC C */
#include <c_asm.h>
static inline unsigned realcc(void) {
    return asm("rpcc %v0");
}
#endif

This will get the lower 32 bits of the cycle counter (the upper 32
bits contain a process-specific counter that you can use to calculate
user time for a specific process). Note that it wraps around every few
seconds, and context switches might disturb the result, so you should
do some sanity checks.

If you need *extremely* short measurements (like 20 cycles), and have
an EV6, you should pass a second argument to rpcc, which will avoid it
being executed too early by the out-of-order mechanism. See the Alpha
Architecture Reference Manual (4th edition) for details.

-- 
	Falk


