> on the command line it'd be (assuming u_minlen was say 5 in the current 
> entry)
> 
> edauth -g -dd default | sed 's/:u_minlen#5:/:u_minlen#8:/g' | edauth -s -dd
> 
> Martin Brodie wrote:
> 
>> How do I really use edauth?
>>
>> when I perform
>>  # edauth -dd default
>>
>> I get the following 2 lines below. I guess this is a line editor I am
>> in, but what commands do I use here.
>>
>>  "/tmp/edaBIguCe" 20 lines, 964 characters
>>  :
>>

The colon (:) represents the prompt from `ex', the default EDITOR for 
edauth.

All you have to do is

:1vi

and you'll switch from ex (line mode) to vi (visual mode).  The 1 brings 
you to the top of the file.  ex and vi are two of the five hard links to 
  the same inode.

