...

 How did Compaq come up
> with the cash to buy DEC in the first place?

Compaq was doing very well on its own back then (Dell was a major competitor
but hadn't yet eaten Compaq's lunch).  Also, DEC was significantly
under-valued (it was sitting on several $billion in cash, for example - but
its management was clearly so incompetent that this detracted significantly
from what would otherwise have been a much higher market value), so Compaq
found whatever additional cash it needed to borrow easy to obtain.

 Why did they pull so many idiotic
> moves?

Most of them came after Eckhard Pfeiffer got the boot:  he was the one who
engineered buying DEC and at least appeared interested in leveraging the
technical assets (Alpha and its operating systems) that came with it,
whereas as soon as Capellas took over he and Shane Robison started planning
Alpha's demise (for reasons which it's very difficult to explain as anything
other than abject stupidity:  Alpha was Compaq's most profitable product
area, and later suggestions that it was unprofitable were simple lies -
though of course had Compaq actually attempted to market and grow the Alpha
business it would have been even more profitable).

> Why did they fall flat just recently?

Compaq was falling from shortly after the time it bought DEC:  Dell was
growing fast (largely at Compaq's expense), Pfeiffer got ousted before
completing his efforts to address the problem, and then Capellas trashed the
enterprise business that kept Compaq afloat for years while it lost money on
PCs.

- bill





