Paul Repacholi wrote:

> No Spam <me@spam.no> writes:
> 
> 
>>Windows NT started as a microkernel, and a lot of code has moved
>>back in to the kernel for performance reasons.  Even MacOS X, which
>>is based on MACH 3.0, moved a lot of stuff back into the kernel.
>>
> 
> Oh. NT is stolen from MICA and that is a re-write of VMS (3.4 based
> I believe). So therefore you consider VMS to be a micro-kernel?


Massively parallel UNIX implementations have roots in uniprocessor
UNIX implementations where only one task can grab the kernel and
once in the kernel, nothing can preempt it.  That doesn't mean they
are the same OS (though they do largely share the same amazingly
poor API.  Go figure...)

As for NT, it had the microkernel model of having the personality
modules with much of the systems oriented work running in separate
userland processes (one for WIN32, one for OS/2 and one for POSIX,)
with all the extra context switching overhead that entailed.  They've
since moved a lot of that back into the kernel (apparently sucking in
things like part of their web server in the process :-)  It also had
SMP and kernel scheduled threads, things VMS didn't get until 5.0 and
(IIRC) 7.0 respecively.  Add in the differences in file systems,
networking stacks, user interface, and other dramatic feature and API
diferences, and it's clear that NT even its original form was a very
different beast from VMS, even if they did share a common ancestory.

As for MICA, I've seen very little about it.  I've heard rumors that
it was a new portable OS designed to replace VMS, but I've never
seen anything published on its architecture, so I can't say if it
had microkernel features or not.

-Steve



