In what library is the malloc you link against with gcc ?

Just a remark, by default,  you do not malloc RAM, but VM. malloc will
enlarge your virtual address space, at least the first time you call
it. but RAM, so Resident Set will be created at first access with Zero
Filled pages.

free will not reduce your Virtual address space, same space created
will be used by next mallocs.

In one case malloc will create Resident Set - IE RAM - at call : in
this mode your process locks his pages in RAM. this is a real time
extension option.


